Tensile and Low Cycle Fatigue (LCF) Testing
Tensile and LCF testing of the VPS Cu-8Cr-4Nb was conducted at Metcut Research. A base line temperature of 538°C(1000°F) was chosen for all samples because it was within the range of prior test temperatures and is comparable to a rocket engine combustion chamber environment. For a comparison to NASA-Glenn work, three tensile and LCF samples made from extruded material were also tested.
Tensile Testing
All tensile tests were performed according to ASTM E-21 test specifications at 538°C (1000°F) with an argon environment to minimize oxidation. Tensile properties for –150/+325, –270, and –325 mesh Cu-8Cr-4Nb vs. HIP time are shown in Figures 4 and 5. The NASA-Glenn extruded material is shown in the 1 hour HIP column for comparison. The results indicate that the VPS Cu-8Cr-4Nb retains good strength at elevated temperatures. This is evidence of the very good stability of the Cr2Nb precipitates and their ability to strengthen Cu even at extreme temperatures. The –270 VPS material provided the highest UTS values. The –150/+325 VPS material retained equivalent strength as the extruded material for all HIP times. As expected, the strength of the materials decreased slightly with increasing HIP time.


